# Vulnerability details

## Summary
Attacker can drain whole ethers in ``FeeSplitter`` contract because ``setCurves()`` function in this contract doesnt have any access control so attacker can call this function and change ``Curves`` contract address to fake ``Curves`` contract that he created and drain whole fees.

## Vulnerability Details
``setCurves()`` function in ``FeeSplitter`` contract is used to set ``Curves`` contract address to able to interact with it and also ``balanceOf()`` function in ``FeeSplitter`` contract calls ``curvesTokenBalance()`` in ``Curves`` contract. attacker can create fake ``Cruves`` contract and manipulate ``curvesTokenBalance()`` function to return a massive amount for himself and then call ``setCurves()`` function and change the address to fake ``Curves`` contract that he created. then call ``claimFees()`` function to drain ethers, because ``claimFees()`` function calls ``updateFeeCredit()`` and ``getClaimableFees()`` functions, and both of these functions have calculations that increase return value. we can see these two functions have some calculations and multiply them to balance which is the attacker manipulated with fake ``Curves`` contract.

```solidity
function updateFeeCredit(address token, address account) internal {
        TokenData storage data = tokensData[token];
        uint256 balance = balanceOf(token, account);
        if (balance > 0) {
            uint256 owed = (data.cumulativeFeePerToken - data.userFeeOffset[account]) * balance;
            data.unclaimedFees[account] += owed / PRECISION;
            data.userFeeOffset[account] = data.cumulativeFeePerToken;
        }
    }
```
