# Vulnerability details

## Description
The `ownedCurvesTokenSubjects` is looped through to know if a token exists in the loop before it is added in the `_addOwnedCurvesTokenSubject` function.

    function _addOwnedCurvesTokenSubject(address owner_, address curvesTokenSubject) internal {
        address[] storage subjects = ownedCurvesTokenSubjects[owner_];
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < subjects.length; i++) {
            if (subjects[i] == curvesTokenSubject) {
                return;
            }
        }
        subjects.push(curvesTokenSubject);
    }

This function is called with `owner_` as the address of the Curves contract anytime a user wants to convert his internal Curve tokens to external Curve tokens. He calls the `withdraw` function which calls `_transfer` before calling `_addOwnedCurvesTokenSubject`.

The `ownedCurvesTokenSubjects[owner_]` array of the Curves contract can be increased maliciously by withdrawing many different Curve tokens. It can also grow over time. When it becomes too large, a lot of gas will be consumed to loop over it which can exceed the max gas a transaction can use on the Form network.

## Impact
Users will spend a lot of gas to convert their internal Curve tokens to external Curve tokens or won't be able to convert at all.


## Tools Used
Manual Analysis

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Use Openzeppelin's EnumerableSet instead to implement the ownedCurvesTokenSubjects array. It can also be used for the `userTokens`  array in FeeSplitter.


## Assessed type

Loop

