# Vulnerability details

The problem is within the updateFeeCredit function, which updates a user's userFeeOffset even if their balance is zero. This could allow users to accrue fees without holding any tokens.

```
function updateFeeCredit(address token, address account) internal {
    TokenData storage data = tokensData[token];
    uint256 balance = balanceOf(token, account);
    if (balance > 0) {
        uint256 owed = (data.cumulativeFeePerToken - data.userFeeOffset[account]) * balance;
        data.unclaimedFees[account] += owed / PRECISION;
        data.userFeeOffset[account] = data.cumulativeFeePerToken;
    }
}
```

### Mitigation

Add a check to ensure that the user's fee offset is only updated if they have a non-zero balance.

```
function updateFeeCredit(address token, address account) internal {
    TokenData storage data = tokensData[token];
    uint256 balance = balanceOf(token, account);
    if (balance == 0) {
        return; // Skip updating the fee offset if the balance is zero
    }
    uint256 owed = (data.cumulativeFeePerToken - data.userFeeOffset[account]) * balance;
    data.unclaimedFees[account] += owed / PRECISION;
    data.userFeeOffset[account] = data.cumulativeFeePerToken;
}
```

### Impact

Without the check, a user who previously held tokens and then transferred all of them away could still benefit from fee distributions made after they no longer hold any tokens.
This could lead to an unfair distribution of fees, where non-token holders receive a portion of the fees at the expense of current token holders.



