# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Assume that the address `alice` has bought her first own Curves token. Now, `bob` can call `buyCurvesToken(alice, 0)` for `0 Ether` by supplying `amount == 0`. As a result, `bob` is added to the array `ownedCurvesTokenSubjects[alice]`,
even though `bob` owns exactly zero Curves tokens by `alice`. This works against the concept of the token growing in price, when more friends are buying it.

If the call `buyCurvesToken(alice, 0)` is done multiple times from different addresses, the array `ownedCurvesTokenSubjects[alice]` grows in size. This increases the gas costs of iterating over `ownedCurvesTokenSubjects[alice]` in [_addOwnedCurvesTokenSubject](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-curves/blob/516aedb7b9a8d341d0d2666c23780d2bd8a9a600/contracts/Curves.sol#L328-L336):

```sol
    function _addOwnedCurvesTokenSubject(address owner_, address curvesTokenSubject) internal {
        address[] storage subjects = ownedCurvesTokenSubjects[owner_];
        for (uint256 i = 0; i < subjects.length; i++) {
            if (subjects[i] == curvesTokenSubject) {
                return;
            }
        }
        subjects.push(curvesTokenSubject);
    }
```

As a result, the benign users would have to pay the increased gas prices for buying the `alice` token. Importantly, there is no way for `alice` to clean up the spam introduced by the attacker. The addresses are only added into `ownedCurvesTokenSubjects` but are never removed.

