## Details
[`FeeSplitter:setCurves` function does not have any access](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-curves/blob/main/contracts/FeeSplitter.sol#L35-L37) controls, allowing anyone to change `curves` address to a malicious address.
`Curves` contract calls [feeSplitter when a user buys or sells curveSubjectTokens](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-curves/blob/main/contracts/Curves.sol#L246-L249), a malicious user can set `FeeSplitter::curves` to an EOA (non-contract) reverting all buy and sell attempts, because `Curves` calls `addFees` function of `FeeSplitter` which then calls `curves` [here](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-curves/blob/main/contracts/FeeSplitter.sol#L43-L46) but since `curves` is an EOA the transaction reverts

## Proof of Concept
As you can see in [this](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-01-curves/blob/main/contracts/FeeSplitter.sol#L35-L37) function, there are no access control checks

    function setCurves(Curves curves_) public {
        curves = curves_;
    }

## Impact:
Unauthorized update of critical states

