## Impact

When `manager` calls `addFees`, the attacker can change `curves` to control the value of `cumulativeFeePerToken`, thereby seeking profits or DoS contracts

## Proof of Concept

Anyone can call this function and the curves can be forged by an attacker

```solidity
    function setCurves(Curves curves_) public {
        curves = curves_;
    }
```

The functions that this function directly affects are `totalSupply`, and the result of this function will affect addFees

`addFees` will be triggered whenever a user buys or sells curveToken

```solidity
    function addFees(address token) public payable onlyManager {
        uint256 totalSupply_ = totalSupply(token);
        if (totalSupply_ == 0) revert NoTokenHolders();
        TokenData storage data = tokensData[token];
        data.cumulativeFeePerToken += (msg.value * PRECISION) / totalSupply_;
    }
```

An attacker can use mev attack to call `setCurves` before calling `addFees` and return a very small `totalSupply_`, such as `totalSupply_ = 1`, then `data.cumulativeFeePerToken` will be increased by `msg.value * 1e18`, This is a huge value, and `cumulativeFeePerToken` is proportional to `unclaimFees` in the function `updateFeeCredit`. The attacker calls `claimFees` to claim the fees in `unclaimedFees`

In addition, the attacker can also make `totalSupply_` return zero, thereby generating a divide-by-zero error and preventing `addFees` from being called successfully.

In all the above cases, the attacker can forge a curves contract to return the desired result because curves can be any value.

