# Vulnerability details

## Description

In the Curves protocol, user interactions involving token purchases or direct transfers trigger the addition of tokens to the `ownedCurvesTokenSubjects` array. The function `_addOwnedCurvesTokenSubject()` manages this process. Its operation includes a check to ensure no duplicate tokens are added:

```solidity
function _addOwnedCurvesTokenSubject(address owner_, address curvesTokenSubject) internal {
    address[] storage subjects = ownedCurvesTokenSubjects[owner_];
    for (uint256 i = 0; i < subjects.length; i++) {
        if (subjects[i] == curvesTokenSubject) {
            return;
        }
    }
    subjects.push(curvesTokenSubject);
}
```

This function can become inefficient and gas-intensive as the array grows, potentially leading to a Denial of Service (DoS). The root cause is the unchecked growth of the array, with tokens continuously added but never removed.

## Impact

As users engage in diverse token trades, the `ownedCurvesTokenSubjects` array expands significantly. In extreme cases, this growth may render the `_addOwnedCurvesTokenSubject()` function inoperable due to excessive gas consumption during array traversal. This dysfunction affects critical functions such as `_transfer()` and `_buyCurvesToken()`, potentially hindering high-volume traders from acquiring new tokens or receiving them via transfers.

## Tools Used

Manual Review.

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

To address this risk, it is advisable to implement a strategy that bypasses the need for iterating over the entire array for each transaction. A viable solution involves introducing a mapping structure to track the tokens currently in the array. Consequently, verifying the presence of a token in the `ownedCurvesTokenSubjects` array would simply involve a mapping lookup, significantly preventing the Denial of Service risks.


