# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The state `ownedCurvesTokenSubjects` stores all curve token subjects that an account owns. Whenever a newly curve token subject is sent to an account, its existence is checked and then it is stored in `ownedCurvesTokenSubjects`. 

However, there is no constraint to check validity of `curveTokenSubjects` in function `Curves#_transfer()` and zero amount is allowed to be transferred. This issue allows an malicious sender to transfer arbitrary `curveTokenSubjects` to a receiver and trigger the logics in `Curves#_addOwnedCurvesTokenSubject()`. By repeating this action, the array length of `ownedCurvesTokenSubjects[account]` is getting increased. And after the length is big enough, the logics in `Curves#_addOwnedCurvesTokenSubject()` will be out of block gas limit. The issue also happens with `Curves#transferAllCurvesTokens()` when the sender has many owned curve token subjects.

This issue also affect the flow of deposit/withdraw when an attacker repeats the withdraw of an arbitrary `curveTokenSubjects` with amount = 0, forcing `ownedCurvesTokenSubjects` for Curve contract address to grow in length. And then, the withdraw/deposit transaction would get DoS

