# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The attacker can get a larger amount of fee.

## Proof of Concept
The `FeeSpliter.sol#setCurves()` is as follows.
```solidity
function setCurves(Curves curves_) public {
    curves = curves_;
}
```
As we see above, there is no access control in this function.

The `FeeSpliter.sol#claimFees()` is as follows.

```solidity
function claimFees(address token) external {
81: updateFeeCredit(token, msg.sender);
82: uint256 claimable = getClaimableFees(token, msg.sender);
83: if (claimable == 0) revert NoFeesToClaim();
    tokensData[token].unclaimedFees[msg.sender] = 0;
    payable(msg.sender).transfer(claimable);
    emit FeesClaimed(token, msg.sender, claimable);
}
```

The value of the claimable is calculated in `L82` as follows.
`claimable = (token.cumulativeFeePerToken - token.userFeeOffset) * balance / PRECISION`
`          = ((token.cumulativeFeePerToken - token.userFeeOffset) * curves.curvesTokenBalance(token, account) * PRECISION) / PRECISION `

To set the value of `curves.curvesTokenBalance(token, account)` to the value intended by the attacker, he creates a MalicioutCurves Contract with the following malicious code,
and calls `FeeSpliter.sol#setCurves()` to set the value of  `FeeSpliter.sol#curves` to this malicious contract address.

```solidity
contract MalicioutCurves {    
    mapping(address => mapping(address => uint256)) public curvesTokenBalance;

    constructor(address token, address account) {
        curvesTokenBalance[token][account] = 1000 // this value can be set by attacker.
    }
}
```

In the above code, the attacker sets the value of `curvesTokenBalance[token][account]` to 1000.
In this way, the attacker sets the value of `curves.curvesTokenBalance(token, account)` to a value greater than his balance and receive a larger amount of fee by calling `FeeSpliter.sol#claimFees()`.

