# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Anyone can call `setCurves` and steal all the fees from the FeeSplitter.

## Proof of Concept

`setCurves` is a function to set the curves of FeeSplitter, and the curves are used to check how many curve tokens a user has when distributing fees.

```solidity
function setCurves(Curves curves_) public {
        curves = curves_;
    }
```

Note that curves is declared as a public function without any modifiers like onlyOwner, so anyone can call it.

So, if malicious user want to exploit this, consider the following scenario.

0. 100 ether is stacked in the FeeSplitter, undistributed.

1. User buy one random curve token.

2. wait for `addFees` to be performed (user will have a `userFeeOffset`).

3. Deploy a contract that returns a very large value for `curvesTokenBalance`.

4. Set that contract to `setCurves`.

5. `claimFees` to capture all the ether.

In the above scenario, the balance is very large, so malicious user can set `getClaimableFees` to return as much as he want. Attacker can use this to steal all the ether in the contract.

```solidity
function getClaimableFees(address token, address account) public view returns (uint256) {
        TokenData storage data = tokensData[token];
        uint256 balance = balanceOf(token, account);
        uint256 owed = (data.cumulativeFeePerToken - data.userFeeOffset[account]) * balance;
        return (owed / PRECISION) + data.unclaimedFees[account];
    }
```

