# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Functions that should only be called by owner and manager can be called by anyone, which can have a huge impact on the protocol.

## Proof of Concept

```solidity
modifier onlyOwner() {
        msg.sender == owner;
        _;
    }
```

onlyOwner is used by setERC20Factory, setFeeRedistributor, and setProtocolFeePercent in Curves.

However, the current implementation does not check the `msg.sender == owner` logic in the require statement, so it is done even if msg.sender is not owner.

As a result, anyone can deploy a malicious ERC20 contract by changing the value of ERC20Factory, manipulate feeRedistributor and protocolFeePercent to maximize protocolFee, and then hijack it.

```solidity
modifier onlyManager() {
        managers[msg.sender] == true;
        _;
    }
```

Security.sol's onlyManager should only be callable when msg.sender is a manager.

However, the current implementation does not check the `managers[msg.sender] == true;` logic in the require statement, so it is performed even if msg.sender is not a manager.

The onlyManager is being used in addFees and onBalanceChange in FeeSplitter and setMaxFeePercent and setExternalFeePercent in Curves.

The above vulnerability can be exploited to call onBalanceChange to delete an arbitrary user's rewards.

Also, setMaxFeePercent and setExternalFeePercent can be used to increase the maximum fee percent by a very large amount and steal funds by taking a fee.

## Tools Used

VS Code

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Add require.

```solidity
modifier onlyOwner() {
        require(msg.sender == owner);
        _;
    }
```

```solidity
modifier onlyManager() {
        require(managers[msg.sender] == true);
        _;
    }
```


