## Proof of Concept

When user claims fess, updateFeeCredit function called to update unclaimed fee amount for user.

```
    function updateFeeCredit(address token, address account) internal {
        TokenData storage data = tokensData[token];
        uint256 balance = balanceOf(token, account);
        if (balance > 0) {
            // @audit this line assumes that user balance is constant 
            // since data.userFeeOffset[account] was last set
            uint256 owed = (data.cumulativeFeePerToken - data.userFeeOffset[account]) * balance;
            data.unclaimedFees[account] += owed / PRECISION;
            data.userFeeOffset[account] = data.cumulativeFeePerToken;
        }
    }
```

cumulativeFeePerToken is a common tehnique for distributing rewards for stakers/holdres and it assumes that these calculatioin done on every balance change. But curves contract not calling FeeSplitter on transfers (= balance changes) at all.

To understant why it's critical, imagine that user is cold account and his `data.userFeeOffset[account] = 0`. After transfering curve shares to him via Curves.transferCurvesToken, owed in above function will be `data.cumulativeFeePerToken * balance` which is non zero and user already eligible for rewards. In fact, this is a big reward as if user held all his balance from very begining.

