# Vulnerability details

## Impact

The Token Subject can make any function dependent on `Curves::_transferFees` function revert by calling `Curves::setReferralFeeDestination` function and setting a contract that has a receive() revert() function.

Thus the following functions: `_buyCurvesToken`, `sellCurvesToken`, ` buyCurvesTokenWithName`, ` buyCurvesTokenForPresale` and ` buyCurvesTokenWhitelisted` will always revert.


## Proof of Concept

1. Alice is a popular vlogger that decided to sell her token to her community using the Curves protocol.
2. She does everything right, mints the token, people starts buying and selling it. A real success.
3. At some point in time, triggered by something, Alice decides that no one should buy or sell her token ever again, whether because she doesn’t want the protocol to earn any fees, or because she was upset by her community, or she wants to create a 3rd party market to bank all the fees/scam her community (using `Curves:transferCurvesToken` which still works).
4. She deploys the following contract:
```javaScript
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity 0.8.7;

contract badBob {
    receive() external payable {
        revert();
    }

    fallback() external {
        revert();
    }
}
``` 
5. She then calls `Curves: setReferralFeeDestination` with the address of the ` badBob` contract as input.
6. From now on anything that involves transferring fees will revert.
6.1 Even if the Manager comes and calls `Curves:setExternalFeePercent` with 0 `referralFeePercent` as an input it won’t stop Alice’s plan because the `referralDefined` variable will still be true, which will make the `Curves::_transferFees` function execute the `referralFeeDestination[curvesTokenSubject].call{value: referralFee}("")` call, which will revert even if the `referralFee` is 0.

