# Vulnerability details

## Impact
In the ``FeeSplitter.sol`` contract when a fee is received, ``cumulativeFeePerToken`` for that token is updated in the ``addFees()`` function. Each user that buys or sells tokens from the protocol also gets the ``userFeeOffset`` associated with his account updated to the latest value of the ``cumulativeFeePerToken`` in the ``onBalanceChange()`` function. When buying or selling tokens, first the ``onBalanceChange()`` function is called. The problem arises because the ``Curves.sol`` contract allows users to transfer their tokens to different addresses and when a new address gets transferred a token, the ``userFeeOffset`` associated with his address is 0. This allows him to claim ``uint256 owed = (data.cumulativeFeePerToken - data.userFeeOffset[account]) * balance;`` which will be the ``cumulativeFeePerToken`` multiplied by the balance, because ``userFeeOffset`` will be 0. A malicious user can transfer and claimFees as many times as he wishes paying only for the gas fees. Keep in mind the same attack vector can be executed either by calling ``transferCurvesToken()`` or ``transferAllCurvesTokens()`` in the ``Curves.sol`` contract, or first withdrawing(converting) the user tokens to ERC20 tokens by calling ``withdraw()``, transferring them to another account utilizing the built in ``transfer()`` function in the ERC20 standard and then depositing them back into the Curves protocol via the ``deposit()`` function in the ``Curves.sol`` contract.

