## Impact
In the ``FeeSpliter.sol`` contract the ``setCurves()`` function is missing an access modifier, thus allowing anyone to set an address for the ``Curves.sol`` contract from where the user balance is read when user rewards are calculated. The missing access control allows an attacker to set up a malicious contract that implements the following ``mapping(address => mapping(address => uint256)) public curvesTokenBalance;`` mapping, which is used to read balances of tokens a user has. In the malicious contract the attacker can manipulate the mapping to show whatever balance of tokens he wants, which can be used to withdraw ``ETH`` rewards from the ``FeeSpliter.sol`` contract. The ``Curves.sol`` contract can also be bricked if the ``curves`` variable in ``FeeSplitter.sol`` points to a contract that doesn't implement the ``curvesTokenBalance()`` function or just simply an EOA. In the POC section I have demonstrated both scenarios.

