# Vulnerability details

## Impact

Funds from `FeeSplitter` can be stolen through a reentrancy vulnerability in the token buy flow:

First, setup a token (i.e., we are `curvesTokenSubject`) and buy a minimal amount of it. Then wait for fees to accumulate in `FeeSplitter` over time due to trading.

The attack can then be performed by buying a large amount of the token (possibly by using a flash loan for funds). In the buy flow, our balance is first increased, then `_transferFees` is called. Inside this method, fees are transferred through a call to us:

```
// ...

(bool success2, ) = curvesTokenSubject.call{value: subjectFee}("");

// ...

if (feesEconomics.holdersFeePercent > 0 && address(feeRedistributor) != address(0)) {
	feeRedistributor.onBalanceChange(curvesTokenSubject, msg.sender);
	feeRedistributor.addFees{value: holderFee}(curvesTokenSubject);
}

``` 

It is important to note that this happens before the calls to `onBalanceChange` and `addFees` are made further down.

When we receive control flow due to the external call to use, we can enter `FeeSplitter.claimFees` which in turn calls `updateFeeCredit`, which checks our current balance and calculates the pending fees. 

```
function updateFeeCredit(address token, address account) internal {
	TokenData storage data = tokensData[token];
	uint256 balance = balanceOf(token, account);
	if (balance > 0) {
		uint256 owed = (data.cumulativeFeePerToken - data.userFeeOffset[account]) * balance;
		data.unclaimedFees[account] += owed / PRECISION;
		data.userFeeOffset[account] = data.cumulativeFeePerToken;
	}
}
```

This balance includes the funds from the purchase we are currently making, meaning these funds are treated as if they had been deposited for a long time, which is not the case. `claimFees` will then pay us a significant amount of fees, thus draining funds from the contract

