        _transfer(curvesTokenSubject, address(this), msg.sender, tokenAmount);
    }

```
An attacker can repeatedly trigger the CurvesERC20(externalToken).burn operation before the internal state of the deposit function is updated, they might be able to exploit the reentrancy vulnerability.

The attacker deposits an amount, triggering the CurvesERC20(externalToken).burn function.
Before the balance update in _transfer, the attacker re-enters the deposit function, triggering another burn operation.
The attacker repeats this process, exploiting the reentrancy vulnerability.
The consequence of such an attack could be multiple burn operations without a corresponding decrease in the internal token balance, leading to potential financial losses for the contract owner.

## Proof of Concept
```
CurvesERC20(externalToken).burn(msg.sender, amount);

```
1. The CurvesERC20(externalToken).burn(msg.sender, amount) call is made before the _transfer function, which changes the internal state by updating token balances.
2. In Ethereum, external calls should typically be the last operation in a function to follow the "checks-effects-interactions" pattern.

