# Vulnerability details

## Impact

- When a subject holder wants to claim his deserved rewards, he can directly call `FeeSplitter.claimFees` function with the address of the subject token, and the claimable rewrds is then calculated via `getClaimableFees` where his claimable rewards are calculated based on the difference between `cumulativeFee` and `userOffset`, so the more this value, the higher will be the claimable amount. This is meant to incentivice subject holders to hold their tokens for longer periods of time:

  ```javascript
      function getClaimableFees(address token, address account) public view returns (uint256) {
          TokenData storage data = tokensData[token];
          uint256 balance = balanceOf(token, account);
          uint256 owed = (data.cumulativeFeePerToken - data.userFeeOffset[account]) * balance;
          return (owed / PRECISION) + data.unclaimedFees[account];
      }
  ```

- But users can exploit `claimFees` function by gaining large direct transfers of a subject tokens (as their `data.userFeeOffset[account]` will not be updated when they receive direct transfers) where they will be able to claim more rewards than they deserve, which will result in draining the splitter contract balance.

