# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Since any caller is able to call `FeeSplitter.setCurve()`, a malicious attacker can first set the curve contract to one attacker has setup, then calls `FeeSplitter.claimFees()` to drain all funds in `FeeSplitter` contract.

## Proof of Concept
1. Attacker sets up `MaliciousCurve` contract
2. Attacker then calls `FeeSplitter.setCruve`, and immediately after, calls `claimFees()`.
3. `FeeSplitter.claimFees()` will call `updateFeeCredit`:
```solidity
    function balanceOf(address token, address account) public view returns (uint256) {
        return curves.curvesTokenBalance(token, account) * PRECISION;
    }


    function updateFeeCredit(address token, address account) internal {
        TokenData storage data = tokensData[token];
        uint256 balance = balanceOf(token, account);
        if (balance > 0) {
            uint256 owed = (data.cumulativeFeePerToken - data.userFeeOffset[account]) * balance;
            data.unclaimedFees[account] += owed / PRECISION;
            data.userFeeOffset[account] = data.cumulativeFeePerToken;
        }
    }
``` 
4. Since `balanceOf` is making a call to `curve` which attacker just set, this can return any value which favors the attacker.
5. Attacker gets transferred with large value of ETH, and drains the contract.

