# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The access control in `Curves.sol` and `Security.sol` is broken due to incorrect coded modifiers. This can cause anyone modify the state of the Curves contract, and potentially compromise the protocol.

## Proof of Concept
In `Security.sol`, the modifiers are coded like this:
```solidity
    modifier onlyOwner() {
        msg.sender == owner;
        _;
    }

    modifier onlyManager() {
        managers[msg.sender] == true;
        _;
    }
```
The ownership check doesn't do anything, since there is no require state or anything else to enforce the sender has correct access. This allows any sender to by able to call functions with such modifier.


## Tools Used
Manual review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Wrap the comparison with require statement, such as:
```solidity
    modifier onlyOwner() {
        require(msg.sender == owner, "Not authorized");
        _;
    }

    modifier onlyManager() {
        require(managers[msg.sender] == true, "Not authorized");
        _;
    }
```





